      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1795699225907,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Speed comes down to the FLOP/B ratio. \n* High ratio: the kernel is compute-bound and can get close to the card's peak math speed.\n* Low ratio: the kernel is memory-bound and waits on global memory.\n\n2. Fast memories raise the ratio.\n * Registers, shared memory and constant memory are much smaller than global memory,\nbut much faster.\n* Data read from them doesn't use global memory bandwidth, so the same math\nneeds fewer global reads.\n* Using them well usually means redesigning the algorithm, not just moving data.\n\n3. Tiling is the main way to do that.\n* Cut input into tiles that fit in shared memory.\n* The threads of a block load a tile together, wait at a barrier, compute from it,\nwait again, then load the next tile.\n* Each value is fetched from global once and then shared, so global reads drop\nby tile width.\n\n4. Fast memory is small, and using it has a cost.\n* How much there is depends on the card.\n* Using too much per block and fewer blocks fit on an SM, so occupancy drops\nand latency hiding gets worse.\n\n5. Tiling isn't only a GPU idea.\n* Any system with small, fast memory needs locality: working on one small\npiece of data at a time. On a CPU, the cache plays the role of shared memory.\n\n6. What this chapter covered.\n* The CUDA memory types, locality and tiling.\n* The tiled matmul kernel using shared memory.\n* Boundary checks for widths that aren't a multiple of tile width.\n* Sizing shared memory at launch, so the kernel can adapt to the card. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Speed comes down to the FLOP/B ratio. \n* High ratio: the kernel is compute-bound and can get close to the card's peak math speed.\n* Low ratio: the kernel is memory-bound and waits on global memory.\n\n2. Fast memories raise the ratio.\n * Registers, shared memory and constant memory are much smaller than global memory,\nbut much faster.\n* Data read from them doesn't use global memory bandwidth, so the same math\nneeds fewer global reads.\n* Using them well usually means redesigning the algorithm, not just moving data.\n\n3. Tiling is the main way to do that.\n* Cut input into tiles that fit in shared memory.\n* The threads of a block load a tile together, wait at a barrier, compute from it,\nwait again, then load the next tile.\n* Each value is fetched from global once and then shared, so global reads drop\nby tile width.\n\n4. Fast memory is small, and using it has a cost.\n* How much there is depends on the card.\n* Using too much per block and fewer blocks fit on an SM, so occupancy drops\nand latency hiding gets worse.\n\n5. Tiling isn't only a GPU idea.\n* Any system with small, fast memory needs locality: working on one small\npiece of data at a time. On a CPU, the cache plays the role of shared memory.\n\n6. What this chapter covered.\n* The CUDA memory types, locality and tiling.\n* The tiled matmul kernel using shared memory.\n* Boundary checks for widths that aren't a multiple of tile width.\n* Sizing shared memory at launch, so the kernel can adapt to the card. ",
